          <article class="archive-item" id="post-31253">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>Attackers&#x27; AI Agents Found a Zero-Day in Medical-Imaging Software and Gained Root Access</h3>
            <p>Suspected China-linked hackers exploited a zero-day in MONAI Label to gain root access via an unauthenticated file write vulnerability.</p>
            <p>Suspected China-linked hackers used AI agents to find a zero-day in MONAI Label and gain root access to a university server. The flaw allows unauthenticated users to write files outside the storage folder. This affects versions 0.8.5 and earlier when running without login.</p>
            <p>The agents mapped the application interface and wrote files in about 11 minutes. They used the same flaw to plant a file that Python runs at startup. This triggered a training feature that loaded the file and connected back to the attacker as root.</p>
            <p>The maintainers fixed the source code but no patched version is available. The flaw is now listed as CVE-2026-133968 with a critical rating. The practical steps are to block untrusted access, enable authentication, and run the server under a limited account.</p>
            <p>We need stronger governance and security controls for agentic AI systems. These systems can autonomously discover and exploit vulnerabilities. This highlights the need for robust AI governance and zero-trust principles in AI operations.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-31235">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-21">21 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>A Single Malicious Webpage Let Researchers Hijack Meta Muse and Access Private Chats</h3>
            <p>A malicious webpage could hijack Meta Muse and access private user data by exploiting browser and kernel vulnerabilities.</p>
            <p>A single malicious webpage could hijack Meta Muse and access private user data without triggering any approval prompts. The researchers demonstrated how an attacker could extract conversation history and stored memories from the agent&#x27;s cloud machine. This shows how even well-protected systems can have blind spots when it comes to AI agents and their interactions with external content.</p>
            <p>The attack chain exploited a flaw in Muse&#x27;s built-in browser and a Linux kernel vulnerability to gain full control of the user&#x27;s cloud environment. This reached beyond the agent&#x27;s intended boundaries, including the credential store and the Sentinel monitoring layer. It highlights how critical it is to secure the entire stack, especially when dealing with agentic AI that operates on cloud infrastructure.</p>
            <p>Sentinel was designed as the final gatekeeper for outbound traffic, but the attack bypassed it entirely. This underscores the need for continuous monitoring and strict access controls. Even with built-in security measures, the attack path shows how quickly an adversary can exploit weaknesses in the chain of trust.</p>
            <p>The research team worked with Meta to fix the vulnerabilities before publishing. This collaboration is a positive sign, but it also reminds us that security is an ongoing process. As AI agents become more integrated into our digital lives, we must ensure they&#x27;re as secure as the systems they interact with.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-31233">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-20">20 November 2026</time> &middot; on Krebsonsecurity</p>
            <h3>CTO of ‘RansomwareHelp’ Indicted for Wire Fraud</h3>
            <p>The CTO of RansomwareHelp was indicted for wire fraud after the FBI uncovered his company&#x27;s scheme to facilitate ransomware payments and siphon funds.</p>
            <p>The case against RansomwareHelp’s CTO underscores the urgent need for rigorous vetting of third-party cybersecurity providers. This isn’t just about fraud—it’s about supply chain security. When a company claims to offer advanced solutions but is actually facilitating ransomware payments, it exposes the vulnerabilities in how we assess and trust these vendors. The FBI’s sting revealed a clear pattern: these actors exploit desperation and lack of technical knowledge to bilk victims.</p>
            <p>The indictment shows how easily these scams operate. By leveraging remote access and manipulating ransomware notes, RansomwareHelp avoided detection while siphoning funds. This highlights the critical importance of due diligence. Organizations must demand transparency, verify claims, and ensure their partners adhere to ethical and legal standards. No shortcuts should be taken when it comes to securing the supply chain.</p>
            <p>These cases are part of a growing trend of legal action against fraudulent cybersecurity practices. The DOJ’s focus on these actors signals a shift toward holding providers accountable. For regulated industries, this means rethinking how we select and monitor third-party services. AI and automation can help, but they can’t replace human oversight and ethical governance. We must build systems that are resilient, transparent, and aligned with real-world security practices.</p>
            <p>The broader lesson is clear: trust must be earned, not assumed. As we embrace agentic AI and advanced security frameworks, the need for robust vetting and governance has never been greater. Let’s push for a culture where accountability is non-negotiable. The stakes are too high to ignore the risks of poor vendor management.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-30883">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-20">20 November 2026</time> &middot; on The Hacker News</p>
            <h3>SonicWall Warns Gen 6 Firewalls Won&#x27;t Get Security Patches, Even for Critical Flaws</h3>
            <p>SonicWall warns Gen 6 firewalls no longer receive security patches for critical flaws leaving them vulnerable to exploitation.</p>
            <p>SonicWall has issued a clear warning about the risks of running Gen 6 firewalls. These devices are no longer receiving security patches, even for critical flaws. This creates a significant exposure, especially with AI-assisted attacks becoming more prevalent. Attackers can now exploit unsupported edge devices faster than ever.</p>
            <p>Unsupported devices are a prime target for threat actors looking to gain initial access. The window between vulnerability disclosure and exploitation has shrunk dramatically. AI and automation help attackers scan the internet, analyze patches, and build exploits quickly. This means exploitation can begin before a patch is even available.</p>
            <p>Modernizing infrastructure is more urgent than ever. Legacy systems like Gen 6 firewalls pose a direct risk to network security. They provide a critical entry point for attackers, offering visibility and access to core systems. Replacing these devices with supported models like Gen 8 is essential to maintaining a secure perimeter.</p>
            <p>The time to act is now. Every day an unsupported firewall remains online increases the risk of a breach. Take immediate steps to inventory and retire these devices. If replacement isn&#x27;t possible, harden the remaining systems with strict access controls and continuous monitoring. One breach can compromise everything.</p>
          </article>
