          <article class="archive-item" id="post-30665">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time> &middot; on Threatlabs netskope</p>
            <h3>ARTEX Now Ships a PHP2Shell Exploit Skill | Netskope Threat Labs</h3>
            <p>ARTEX&#x27;s PHP2Shell exploit turns file-inclusion vulnerabilities into remote code execution with minimal human intervention.</p>
            <p>ARTEX’s ability to autonomously exploit PHP2Shell vulnerabilities underscores a critical gap in how we govern agentic AI tools. These skills turn file-inclusion findings into remote code execution, often with minimal human intervention. The speed at which these exploits are weaponized highlights the need for stronger control and oversight.</p>
            <p>The PHP2Shell skill’s orchestration of LFI to RCE is alarming. It leverages session.upload_progress to inject code and execute it server-side. This level of automation reduces the barrier to entry for threat actors and amplifies the risk of widespread compromise. Governance must evolve to address this new threat vector.</p>
            <p>Defenders face a tough choice: patch or mitigate. Patching to supported PHP versions is the only full fix, but for those unable to act quickly, disabling session.upload_progress is a temporary shield. The key takeaway is to treat any file-inclusion finding as a critical alert, given the potential for rapid escalation.</p>
            <p>The broader implication is that agentic AI tools like ARTEX are reshaping the threat landscape. Their ability to turn vulnerabilities into exploits with minimal effort demands a rethinking of how we secure and audit these systems. Governance, audit, and control frameworks must keep pace with the evolution of these tools.</p>
