          <article class="archive-item" id="post-30663">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time> &middot; on Stepsecurity</p>
            <h3>Microsoft agent-framework PyPI Package Compromised</h3>
            <p>The Microsoft agent-framework PyPI package was compromised through a supply chain attack that exfiltrated credentials and attempted to mint identity tokens.</p>
            <p>The recent compromise of the Microsoft agent-framework PyPI package underscores the critical need for robust dependency management and secure CI/CD pipelines. Attackers leveraged a .pth startup hook to execute a credential-stealing payload during Python initialization, without requiring an explicit import. This method highlights how supply chain attacks can bypass traditional security controls.</p>
            <p>The attack unfolded in under 33 minutes, with malicious versions published and quarantined rapidly. This speed suggests the attacker had persistent access to the publishing credentials, emphasizing the risks of unmonitored package uploads. Even after the first release was quarantined, the attacker continued, showing the importance of real-time monitoring and immediate response.</p>
            <p>The payload harvested environment variables, credential files, and live process environments, exfiltrating data to a free webhook endpoint. It also attempted to mint fresh identity tokens from cloud providers, demonstrating the breadth of potential damage. This reinforces the need for continuous monitoring and strict access controls across all CI/CD environments.</p>
            <p>Strict dependency management, secure package validation, and rapid incident response are essential. Organizations must ensure their pipelines are hardened against such attacks. The incident serves as a stark reminder that even trusted packages can be compromised, and vigilance is key to mitigating risk.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="archive-item" id="post-30662">
            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-11-18">18 November 2026</time> &middot; on SafeDep</p>
            <h3>Microsoft agent-framework Compromised Twice on PyPI</h3>
            <p>The Microsoft agent-framework was compromised twice on PyPI allowing credential theft through malicious packages that exploited lack of cryptographic attestation and supply chain vulnerabilities.</p>
            <p>The Microsoft agent-framework compromise on PyPI is a stark reminder of the risks in package repositories. A malicious release stole credentials from any environment that installed it, including CI jobs. The payload ran automatically at Python startup, making detection difficult. This highlights the need for strict dependency hygiene and version pinning in all environments.</p>
            <p>The attack exploited the lack of cryptographic attestation for packages, making it impossible to verify the source. SafeDep flagged the malicious versions quickly, but removing one did not stop the attacker. This shows how critical it is to lock the dependency tree with hashes and avoid relying on a single version control point.</p>
            <p>Secure CI/CD practices are essential. Dependency management should include pinning, hashing, and regular audits. The incident also underscores the importance of monitoring for unexpected maintainers and ensuring that only trusted accounts have publishing access. Every secret exposed by these packages is a potential breach.</p>
            <p>This incident is not isolated. It follows a similar attack on another Microsoft package, showing a pattern of supply chain vulnerabilities. Teams must treat all package dependencies as potential attack vectors. Implementing Zero Trust principles and continuous monitoring can help mitigate these risks. Always verify the integrity of your dependencies.</p>
