            <h3>Lessons from Telegram&#x27;s RCE: The Proper Way to Secure a JavaScript Runtime</h3>
            <p>Running untrusted code in messaging platforms is a classic security challenge, and Telegram’s recent RCE vulnerabilities highlight the risks.</p>
            <p>Running untrusted code in messaging platforms is a classic security challenge, and Telegram’s recent RCE vulnerabilities highlight the risks. The problem isn’t just about code execution—it’s about how that code interacts with the host environment. The same issues apply to agentic AI systems where code runs in isolated contexts.</p>
            <p>The initial approach relied on seccomp as a denylist, but that’s not enough. A compromised engine can bypass these rules and access deeper system resources. The real solution is to isolate the runtime completely, run it in a dedicated process, and enforce strict boundaries. This way, any bug or exploit stays contained within the widget and doesn’t affect the host application.</p>
            <p>Modern approaches like WebAssembly and RLBox help enforce these boundaries. By compiling QuickJS to WebAssembly, we can restrict memory access and control indirect calls. This makes it harder for attackers to exploit memory corruption bugs. Combined with SFI and explicit validation, we shift the risk from the host to the isolated runtime.</p>
            <p>The lesson here is clear: trust no code by default. Assume the engine is compromised and design for what comes next. Whether it’s a messaging platform or an agentic AI system, the same principles apply. Isolate, confine, validate, and keep host capabilities minimal. That’s how you turn an engine exploit into a contained failure.</p>
