            <p>Security teams face growing challenges in managing risks due to accelerating threats and the limitations of current testing and remediation practices.</p>
            <p>The security backlog is a ticking time bomb. AI-driven attacks are accelerating, and the time between discovery and exploitation is shrinking. Fixing findings once a year isn’t enough. Attackers are already moving faster, and your defenses need to keep pace. A 12-month testing cycle gives them free moves before you respond. It’s a tempo problem. Boards and insurers are asking how often environments are tested and how risks are prioritized. Answering “once, back in Q2” isn’t enough. Testing needs to track changes in the environment, not just a calendar date.</p>
            <p>Fixing a ticket doesn’t mean the risk is gone. Automated remediation is rising, but marking a finding as resolved only tells you a change was made. Did the exposure go away? Will it return? Did the fix cause other issues? These are separate questions. In my testing days, we’d retest “closed” items and find them still open or exploitable via a different technique. A patch might pass the scanner but fail real-world validation. Every finding needs two answers: who’s fixing it, and is it really fixed?</p>
            <p>CVSS scores are misleading, and NIST’s decision to stop enriching most CVEs has forced teams to rethink how they prioritize findings. Severity alone isn’t enough. In 2027, I expect more programs to rank findings by exploitability, asset value, and business impact. Context is king. It comes from your own data, not a universal scoring system. Teams are starting to realize that a universal score isn’t enough. You need to know what matters most to your business.</p>
            <p>Focus on ROI. Security tools are multiplying, but how well can you show risk reduction? Spreadsheets are the duct tape of business processes and pose significant risk. They aren’t centralized and lack detail needed for remediation. Tracking fixes is manual and leads to burnout. In 2027, CFOs will ask whether security spending made the company safer. Answering requires pulling findings from every source, collapsing duplicates, tying each to an asset and owner, and following it from discovery to verified fix.</p>
