            <p class="archive-meta"><time datetime="2026-10-26">26 October 2026</time> &middot; on Dark Reading</p>
            <h3>Redefining Security for the Agentic Enterprise</h3>
            <p>AI agents face security risks due to potential unauthorized access and actions, requiring strict access controls, continuous validation, and adaptive containment strategies.</p>
            <p>AI agents find security debt faster than any attacker. Securing them takes task scoped access, quick defense, adaptive containment and safer code.</p>
            <p>Zero trust enforces least privilege access, ensuring every agent has only the permissions it needs. An agent doing one task should never inherit standing access to every repository and database its human user can reach. And this doesn’t end after the first API handshake. We need to continuously validate the identity and authorization at each tool call, keep the agent and its human creator as separate identities, and continuously audit outbound requests that the agent makes.</p>
            <p>Defend at machine speed. No enterprise has enough security analysts to inspect millions of autonomous transactions manually. We need AI working on the defense, too. Let’s say that an agent suddenly starts querying tables it has never touched. A defensive agent can spot the change, match it against the session&#x27;s history and quarantine the session in seconds, within limits set in advance.</p>
            <p>Build containment that adapts. Decoy credentials provide an early tripwire for rogue discovery loops. When an agent attempts to touch a dummy key that serves no operational purpose, the contact should trigger an automatic response: stop the run, revoke the agent&#x27;s access in every environment, and rotate any credentials it could have reached.</p>
          </article>
