            <p>The Mior attack exploits a flaw in AMD SEV-SNP by manipulating Infinity Fabric to redirect memory accesses and bypass PSP checks compromising guest memory integrity.</p>
            <p>The Mior attack exposes a critical flaw in how AMD SEV-SNP handles memory routing. By manipulating the Infinity Fabric, attackers can redirect memory accesses to device memory, bypassing the PSP&#x27;s initialization checks. This allows them to corrupt the RMP and undermine the integrity of guest memory. It’s a stark reminder that even trusted execution environments are vulnerable if we don’t rigorously validate hardware and firmware configurations.</p>
            <p>The attack relies on the PSP’s reliance on a memory map that doesn’t accurately reflect the fabric’s routing. By using device memory to satisfy read-back checks, the attacker fools the system into thinking everything is normal. This highlights a deeper issue: the need to ensure all components align in their routing configurations. Without this, we leave gaps in our security posture.</p>
            <p>As we build cloud infrastructure, we must prioritize the validation of every layer, from the hardware up. Trusted execution environments are only as strong as the assumptions we make about their integrity. Mior shows that even small misconfigurations can have major consequences. We need to push for more transparent and consistent validation across the entire stack.</p>
